        "original_id": "8a74082a-1129-5043-bd16-dae685550c41",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jean-Paul Sartre, George Bernard Shaw, Alan Jay Lerner, Frederick Loewe\n- Works: Nausea, My Fair Lady, Pygmalion\n- Roles: philosopher, playwright, novelist, political activist, biographer, literary critic, critic, polemicist\n\nFacts:\n- George Bernard Shaw was born on 26 July 1856 and died on 2 November 1950.\n- George Bernard Shaw, who insisted on being called Bernard Shaw, was an Irish playwright, critic, and polemicist whose influence on Western theatre, culture, and politics lasted from the 1880s through and beyond his lifetime.\n- Jean-Paul Sartre was born on 21 June 1905 and died on 15 April 1980.\n- Jean-Paul Sartre was a French philosopher, playwright, novelist, political activist, biographer, and literary critic.\n- Nausea, published in 1938 as La Nausée, is a philosophical novel by the existentialist philosopher Jean-Paul Sartre.\n- My Fair Lady is a musical based on George Bernard Shaw's Pygmalion, with a book and lyrics by Alan Jay Lerner and music by Frederick Loewe.",
